WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:04.219
Let's begin and start talking about the stage setup

2
00:00:04.219 --> 00:00:06.500
and the various pieces of hardware

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.859
that you'll be utilizing in a common LED volume.

4
00:00:09.859 --> 00:00:13.179
First off, there's some certain configurations

5
00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:16.620
and considerations that you should take into account.

6
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.640
When LED volumes were first thought of and considered,

7
00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:23.500
they kind of went through a bit of an evolution.

8
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.379
We started off with some various flat configurations,

9
00:00:26.379 --> 00:00:28.440
kind of like what we have here at the LED,

10
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.879
at the latte stage here in Culver City.

11
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.759
These flat configurations were basically flat walls

12
00:00:36.759 --> 00:00:38.719
with right angles,

13
00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:42.359
and they were really kind of difficult to light.

14
00:00:42.359 --> 00:00:44.240
Obviously, when you're dealing with a flat wall

15
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:45.399
and you're standing here,

16
00:00:45.399 --> 00:00:47.240
you're getting a lot of this backlight

17
00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.539
on the actor itself.

18
00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:53.640
So this became a bit of an issue and problematic.

19
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.159
So this heavy backlight required a lot of

20
00:00:56.159 --> 00:00:57.479
bright practical lights,

21
00:00:57.479 --> 00:01:01.179
which caused issues with spill on the stage themselves.

22
00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:04.989
And it also introduced potential problems

23
00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:08.109
for various issues with dealing

24
00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:12.189
with environmental reflections on the stage.

25
00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:14.989
So the stage evolved basically

26
00:01:14.989 --> 00:01:17.469
into a more curved configuration.

27
00:01:17.469 --> 00:01:21.269
And this first started out by having purely cylindrical,

28
00:01:21.269 --> 00:01:26.269
like 100% nearly round stage configurations,

29
00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:32.189
which worked, but they had specific acoustical problems.

30
00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:34.709
Completely round stages,

31
00:01:34.709 --> 00:01:38.629
the sound within the stage was very pronounced

32
00:01:38.629 --> 00:01:40.430
and it echoed everywhere.

33
00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:42.469
So the stage kind of evolved

34
00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:45.670
into a more of a horseshoe shape or U shape.

35
00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:49.430
And this is where we see the majority of most stages

36
00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:52.609
today in their stage of evolution.

37
00:01:52.609 --> 00:01:56.030
The horseshoe shape allowed for the reduction

38
00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.469
of echo and acoustical problems.

39
00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:01.629
They were reduced with the horseshoe design

40
00:02:01.629 --> 00:02:06.230
and they introduced the concept of including a plug wall,

41
00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:08.750
which is basically a wall that could either come down

42
00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.389
like a garage door or rotated in from the sides

43
00:02:12.389 --> 00:02:15.490
to seal the entire environment

44
00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:19.539
to allow for full 360 degree shooting.

45
00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:23.560
When we're considering a location for an actual stage,

46
00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:24.479
here are some things

47
00:02:24.479 --> 00:02:26.780
that you should really pay attention to.

48
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.759
Number one, the stage should have,

49
00:02:28.759 --> 00:02:30.319
and the building that you're planning

50
00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:32.000
on building this stage in,

51
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.280
there should be a very clear span up in the ceiling.

52
00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.080
The ceiling height is critical and important

53
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.080
for being able to have something able or multi-story

54
00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.280
that you might want to shoot.

55
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.800
But in addition to the height of the ceiling

56
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.319
and not having anything that's obstructing the ceiling

57
00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:53.080
in the various spans,

58
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.280
you need to make sure that the ceiling is capable

59
00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.800
of supporting a great amount of weight.

60
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.340
Because depending upon how you configure your stage,

61
00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.759
whether you're setting it up a hanging truss

62
00:03:04.759 --> 00:03:07.879
or if you're having it actually configured

63
00:03:07.879 --> 00:03:09.180
to sit on the ground,

64
00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.759
the ceiling weight capacity is going to be a big deal.

65
00:03:13.759 --> 00:03:15.500
And the fact that you're gonna need

66
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.699
to have really high impact and dense concrete

67
00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:22.659
for the flooring is going to be necessary.

68
00:03:22.659 --> 00:03:24.979
Any kind of vibration that might be introduced

69
00:03:24.979 --> 00:03:27.240
from the flooring of moving objects around

70
00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.319
can potentially destabilize and or require you

71
00:03:31.319 --> 00:03:36.159
to calibrate your motion capture cameras multiple times,

72
00:03:36.159 --> 00:03:38.819
especially if you're moving the large plug wall,

73
00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:41.340
it can really cause a lot of various vibrations.

74
00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.659
So the floors have to be made

75
00:03:43.659 --> 00:03:48.659
of a very strong high impact concrete to absorb vibration.

76
00:03:49.419 --> 00:03:50.259
You need to make sure

77
00:03:50.259 --> 00:03:52.240
that your facility has adequate power.

78
00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:56.259
Obviously when you're dealing with so many LED panels

79
00:03:56.259 --> 00:03:58.419
and you've got lighting and you've got HVAC

80
00:03:58.419 --> 00:04:00.460
and you've got computers,

81
00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.500
your building has to have sufficient power

82
00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.219
to be able to drive all of this.

83
00:04:04.219 --> 00:04:06.360
Soundproofing of course is important.

84
00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:10.379
The issue of course with the wall acoustically

85
00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:12.620
is difficult enough as it is.

86
00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:16.300
If you're in some sort of facility that isn't soundproof,

87
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:17.899
you're going to have problems

88
00:04:17.939 --> 00:04:21.259
and your audio technicians will not be happy about it.

89
00:04:21.259 --> 00:04:23.819
Air conditioning, big issue.

90
00:04:23.819 --> 00:04:25.740
The panels give off heat.

91
00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:29.019
Certainly they give off less heat than practical lights,

92
00:04:29.019 --> 00:04:31.259
but they still generate heat

93
00:04:31.259 --> 00:04:35.360
and all the various computer systems associated do as well.

94
00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.279
So if you don't have good HVAC systems

95
00:04:38.279 --> 00:04:40.500
to keep your environment cool,

96
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.379
you could potentially run into problems or issues there.

97
00:04:44.699 --> 00:04:47.899
You should also have obviously a series of offices

98
00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:49.779
for your production staff.

99
00:04:49.779 --> 00:04:51.980
You need an area to be a mill space

100
00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:55.360
to help construct practical set pieces

101
00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.500
outside of the actual volume itself

102
00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.980
that you can eventually move on to the volume.

103
00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:02.060
And of course, you're going to need some parking

104
00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:05.480
for your facility because running these types of stages

105
00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:07.620
do require a number of personnel.

106
00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.040
So make sure that you have good parking for them.

107
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.399
The stages can be configured

108
00:05:12.399 --> 00:05:13.740
in a number of different ways.

109
00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.379
We've just simplified it here

110
00:05:15.379 --> 00:05:18.300
and we're showing a large stage configuration,

111
00:05:18.939 --> 00:05:19.779
a medium stage configuration

112
00:05:19.779 --> 00:05:21.860
and a small stage configuration.

113
00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:24.420
The NAND stage, one of our partners

114
00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.180
has a facility in El Segundo, California.

115
00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:28.720
This is a fairly large stage.

116
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:31.180
It's 50 foot wide by 30, or excuse me,

117
00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:35.019
53 feet in length and 20 feet high.

118
00:05:35.019 --> 00:05:37.000
It does have a ceiling element

119
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.379
and it has two plug walls that basically rotate

120
00:05:40.379 --> 00:05:42.259
in front of the stage itself

121
00:05:42.259 --> 00:05:46.040
for full 360 degree shooting.

122
00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:49.079
Now the Epic R&D stage,

123
00:05:49.079 --> 00:05:51.540
we consider this more of a medium stage,

124
00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:55.399
31 feet in width, 23 feet in length

125
00:05:55.399 --> 00:05:57.459
and 13 feet in height.

126
00:05:57.459 --> 00:05:58.839
It does have a ceiling

127
00:05:58.839 --> 00:06:01.720
and it does have separate wild walls

128
00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:05.839
that can be moved into place for reflective elements

129
00:06:05.839 --> 00:06:09.000
and or providing additional lighting.

130
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.160
It's 174 degrees,

131
00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:14.920
but it also includes five flat columns of panels.

132
00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:19.920
And this as a stage can certainly do a great job

133
00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.879
for shooting a number of different elements.

134
00:06:22.879 --> 00:06:27.420
It's not quite as encompassing as the large stage

135
00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:30.800
and it doesn't have an actual raised floor.

136
00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.600
So you do have to be a little bit more careful

137
00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.800
as to how you manage that seam transition

138
00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.519
from the stage itself to the floor.

139
00:06:39.519 --> 00:06:42.240
But this would be a medium sized stage.

140
00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:44.680
And then finally, the small stage here

141
00:06:45.319 --> 00:06:48.240
in Culver City, we have our latte stage.

142
00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.680
This is more of like a technical

143
00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:51.680
or a tactical training stage.

144
00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:56.680
It's 16 feet wide by eight feet in height.

145
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.779
And we also have a right angled wall to us as well here.

146
00:07:00.779 --> 00:07:05.910
And we have a large television screen as a wild wall.

147
00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:12.329
This is just a 4K high resolution TV screen

148
00:07:12.329 --> 00:07:15.389
that can provide you with some extra reflections

149
00:07:15.389 --> 00:07:21.240
and some potential additional lighting if necessary.

150
00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.040
Now for a part of equipment,

151
00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:24.680
there's a whole series of equipment

152
00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:26.860
that we need to talk about.

153
00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:28.779
We're gonna go through it first in the slides

154
00:07:28.779 --> 00:07:32.740
and then we'll actually show you the latte stage

155
00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.540
here in Culver City.

156
00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:37.399
Okay, so first is the control cards.

157
00:07:37.399 --> 00:07:40.220
These control cards are basically fundamental

158
00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:42.980
in the actual volume itself.

159
00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:46.019
It's kind of where the traditional brain bar

160
00:07:46.019 --> 00:07:48.740
would be located, even though that term

161
00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.259
isn't really quite utilized anymore.

162
00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:53.079
It's kind of going out of fashion.

163
00:07:53.079 --> 00:07:58.079
The control cards basically house your various stations

164
00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:01.300
that are controlling Unreal Engine.

165
00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:06.300
And you'll have one for the actual control of the engine.

166
00:08:06.939 --> 00:08:10.939
You'll have one probably for secondary purposes

167
00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:15.699
like a multi-purpose cart for helping prep

168
00:08:15.699 --> 00:08:20.699
and set up various shots in preparation.

169
00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:24.209
They're not cheap.

170
00:08:24.209 --> 00:08:29.209
They can range anywhere from 5,000 to $7,500 per cart,

171
00:08:30.209 --> 00:08:32.879
but they're really great in the sense

172
00:08:32.879 --> 00:08:34.899
that they can be moved around on stage,

173
00:08:34.899 --> 00:08:36.820
relocated and repositioned

174
00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:39.320
depending upon your configuration and needs.

175
00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:42.649
Next, we have our Q-Take system

176
00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:44.370
and our Mac Pro.

177
00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:45.649
I just want to also point out,

178
00:08:45.649 --> 00:08:50.450
these are the carts and devices

179
00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:55.129
that we're using at the LA R&D stage in El Segundo.

180
00:08:55.129 --> 00:08:57.330
So we have a Q-Take system.

181
00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.090
This is basically a video assist machine

182
00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:03.450
that records everything that's being captured on the camera.

183
00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:05.870
It is a Mac-based system,

184
00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:10.250
and it's a pretty expensive device.

185
00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:13.370
It's around $100,000 for this entire system,

186
00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:17.850
but it does handle all of the recording and playback

187
00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:19.850
and video assist for productions

188
00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:22.340
that are being conducted on stage.

189
00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:26.860
Next, we have the ThinkLogical 10 gigabit

190
00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:29.580
KVM switching matrix.

191
00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:31.779
This is basically,

192
00:09:31.779 --> 00:09:36.889
this allows us to basically relocate our CPUs

193
00:09:36.889 --> 00:09:39.090
into a cooled machine room.

194
00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:42.850
And then this device connects up to those machines

195
00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:45.090
so that you can have your keyboard,

196
00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:46.850
video and mouse controls

197
00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:49.250
routed to a different portion of your stage.

198
00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:51.490
And we basically have this set up

199
00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.129
for all the individual carts.

200
00:09:53.129 --> 00:09:55.529
All the carts within the stage

201
00:09:55.529 --> 00:09:58.129
have the ability to switch between different machines

202
00:09:58.129 --> 00:09:59.370
that they're controlling

203
00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:01.809
and the video that's coming into the monitors.

204
00:10:01.809 --> 00:10:04.370
So you're going to need something that'll help

205
00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:07.629
to route your keyboard and video and mouse functions.

206
00:10:07.629 --> 00:10:09.350
There's the switch price

207
00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:12.169
and then all the individual extensions.

208
00:10:12.169 --> 00:10:13.350
It can add up,

209
00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:17.309
but it's really an important piece to have

210
00:10:17.309 --> 00:10:21.169
so you can keep a lot of the machines off stage

211
00:10:21.169 --> 00:10:25.019
and in a more cooled machine room.

212
00:10:25.019 --> 00:10:27.419
Now, speaking of computer systems,

213
00:10:27.419 --> 00:10:28.820
we have a number of them.

214
00:10:29.799 --> 00:10:33.399
I talked about the control cart and the control machines.

215
00:10:33.399 --> 00:10:36.720
Well, these are a series of AMD workstations

216
00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:39.679
that are prepped and loaded

217
00:10:39.679 --> 00:10:44.279
to basically ingest and bring in Unreal Engine content

218
00:10:44.279 --> 00:10:46.480
and prepare it for the stage

219
00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:48.519
in order to get it ready for use.

220
00:10:48.519 --> 00:10:50.080
So these machines,

221
00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:53.700
just their base price is probably about $5,000,

222
00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:57.120
but it goes way up past that.

223
00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.200
You've got to add in additional memory,

224
00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:00.740
various hard drives

225
00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:04.080
and a really decent graphics card is necessary

226
00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:05.759
for these control machines.

227
00:11:05.799 --> 00:11:09.720
Now, these control machines do not actually send

228
00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:12.360
and transact data up onto the wall,

229
00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.240
but you still need to have a powerful graphics card

230
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:18.480
inside of these machines

231
00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:22.019
in order to basically prep and set up

232
00:11:22.019 --> 00:11:24.480
what will be transacted to the wall.

233
00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:29.480
So probably would recommend the higher RTX Titan machines

234
00:11:29.559 --> 00:11:33.519
or 30, it could be 30 90s or 40 90s

235
00:11:33.519 --> 00:11:37.779
or any of the Quadro based graphics cards are great too.

236
00:11:37.779 --> 00:11:40.820
Anything with a lot of video card memory

237
00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:43.879
is probably the best choice.

238
00:11:43.879 --> 00:11:46.559
Next, there's a whole series of render computers.

239
00:11:46.559 --> 00:11:48.720
These are also AMD workstations.

240
00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:51.639
Again, base price $5,000,

241
00:11:51.639 --> 00:11:53.240
but probably even more,

242
00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:56.799
more in the neighborhood of 15 to $20,000

243
00:11:56.799 --> 00:11:58.120
once they're fully decked out

244
00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:01.740
with all the necessary RAM and hard drives and so forth.

245
00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:06.139
But they are also equipped with two highly specific GPUs.

246
00:12:06.139 --> 00:12:09.580
The A6000 typically is what we prefer.

247
00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:13.879
This is a Quadro based card

248
00:12:13.879 --> 00:12:16.740
that's capable of handling synchronization,

249
00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:19.740
which is important, mandatory even

250
00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:22.080
for the whole LED process.

251
00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.000
You're gonna have two of these GPUs

252
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.899
in each individual machine

253
00:12:26.899 --> 00:12:30.379
and they are basically linked together via SLI

254
00:12:30.379 --> 00:12:32.620
so that the two graphics cards

255
00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:34.059
are talking with one another

256
00:12:34.059 --> 00:12:38.320
and that factors in to how Unreal Engine draws

257
00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:40.840
the content on the screen,

258
00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:43.580
what's inside of the frustum and what's outside.

259
00:12:44.899 --> 00:12:48.679
I mentioned the GPU, there's the Nvidia A6000 card.

260
00:12:48.679 --> 00:12:53.679
This is basically about $5,500 per graphics card.

261
00:12:53.679 --> 00:12:56.779
It needs to be combined with the Quadro Sync 2 card,

262
00:12:56.779 --> 00:13:00.519
which is the card it utilizes for gen locking purposes.

263
00:13:00.519 --> 00:13:05.100
So gen locking is a big part of this equation,

264
00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:07.340
something that we'll talk about in just a moment,

265
00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:10.179
but you need to have not only those two graphics cards

266
00:13:10.179 --> 00:13:13.200
in these machines, but also the Quadro Sync card,

267
00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:16.539
which will then link to every other rendering machine

268
00:13:16.539 --> 00:13:19.409
in your machine room.

269
00:13:19.409 --> 00:13:21.850
All right, other potential hardware and video gear

270
00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:23.570
that you're going to need,

271
00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:27.330
timecode, timecode sync, gen lock.

272
00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:30.529
These are, again, critical components.

273
00:13:30.529 --> 00:13:33.850
In the R&D stage, we have the Everett's

274
00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:35.889
and Brainstorm solutions.

275
00:13:35.889 --> 00:13:39.370
These basically, again, provide house timecode

276
00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:42.129
and house gen lock to all the devices

277
00:13:42.129 --> 00:13:45.590
that are either in the machine room,

278
00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:47.730
like your render machines, your control machines

279
00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:50.889
and so forth, but then they also supply gen lock

280
00:13:50.889 --> 00:13:53.330
to your motion capture cameras

281
00:13:54.169 --> 00:13:58.090
and also to your video assist in recording

282
00:13:58.090 --> 00:14:00.169
and the actual camera that you're using itself

283
00:14:00.169 --> 00:14:01.169
to film all of this.

284
00:14:01.169 --> 00:14:04.389
So critical component, you have to have

285
00:14:04.389 --> 00:14:07.830
some kind of timecode and gen lock generation.

286
00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:11.710
We also have an HDMI matrix router.

287
00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:14.389
Now this is the Lightware MX2.

288
00:14:14.389 --> 00:14:17.710
This is a zero latency HDMI video router.

289
00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:21.909
Now, all of the A6000 cards in your render machines,

290
00:14:21.909 --> 00:14:24.669
they all have display ports out of them.

291
00:14:24.669 --> 00:14:28.470
So this will take the display port

292
00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:31.590
and convert it into an HDMI signal

293
00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:34.590
and allow you to route the video output

294
00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:36.230
from any of your render machines

295
00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:38.549
wherever you need it to go.

296
00:14:38.549 --> 00:14:43.799
So this basically combines and sends all this information

297
00:14:43.879 --> 00:14:47.360
to your megapixel or any other brand

298
00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:50.039
that you might be using of your LED processor.

299
00:14:50.039 --> 00:14:54.159
So it converts display port into HDMI

300
00:14:54.159 --> 00:14:58.419
and that signal is then sent to your LED processors.

301
00:14:58.419 --> 00:15:01.000
This is a fairly expensive piece of equipment.

302
00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:06.000
It's about $50,000, but it's pretty critical to have.

303
00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:06.960
If you don't have it,

304
00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:10.220
you're gonna have to be sending individual signals

305
00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:11.759
from each individual machine

306
00:15:11.759 --> 00:15:14.500
and that's a lot of extra cabling.

307
00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:18.039
This simplifies a lot.

308
00:15:18.039 --> 00:15:20.039
Now, the next item that we have on this slide

309
00:15:20.899 --> 00:15:22.799
is the HDMI distribution and monitoring system.

310
00:15:22.799 --> 00:15:26.080
This is a bit of a luxury item, quite frankly.

311
00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:28.159
This is the Barco E2.

312
00:15:28.159 --> 00:15:31.879
This basically combines a number of multiple video signals

313
00:15:31.879 --> 00:15:34.600
into a single signal, and that's hard to say,

314
00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:38.759
that is then projected for your stage operators to see.

315
00:15:38.759 --> 00:15:42.519
So these are $85,000 a piece,

